[{:seon.task/name "a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the printer audit (docs/render.md; owner, 2026-10-25: \"focus on not generating crap and then not rendering crap\"). An entry of ```seon.problems/health``` carries keys that say nothing, required by :seon.problems/row: `:producer nil`, `:at-ms nil`, `:proofs []`, `:answered-by []` on every derived entry (```seon.problems/derived-row```, the entry function in ```seon.problems/unnamed-sites```), and repeats itself: a recorded error's entry (```seon.problems/error-row```) holds :example, :signature, :count and :agents both at its top and in :evidence. Printed raw (a value that lost its schema's metadata, e.g. (:classes (my.root/problems sys))), the 177 :stale-section entries were 13 keys each, most of them empty. Correct: an entry holds a key only when it knows its value (the schema's keys optional, nil meaning unknown never stored), and :evidence only what the entry's own keys do not say; its readers (the done and merge gates, seon.render.ui/problems-model, seon.render.ui/problem-row) read a missing key as none. Proof: every entry of health on a seeded db has no nil or empty value and no :evidence key equal to its own."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows}}
 {:seon.task/name "closed-names-no-legacy-subject"
  :seon.task/plan "The fallback this task named is gone (093ace07cf): `seon.problems/closed?` reads only an error's :seon.error/subject, and a dead citation is refused at its write, so no :seon.doc/refused error is recorded any more. What remains: a test that every recorded error class names a subject at its producer (no such test exists; `seon.problems-test` has only the one that a subject closes an error when it changes), and the standing store's old entries without a subject (at least db 1795, task file my.task.edn not readable EDN, and db 1979, git add -A unable to stat a path), which only the owner's go retracts (one write through seon_eval root) unless runs/store is erased, in which case the test is all."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/every-recorded-error-class-names-seon-error-subject}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-measure-past-its-bound-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03: \"If we put in bounds we could have problems detect themselves. The point is to passively update it progressively as the system runs rather than do a poll of the current system\"; and before, on health: \"We are supposed to be passively collecting data and problems\" (agent evals, turn times, new agents). What exists: the measures are recorded as facts at their writes: an eval's :ms :ticks :bytes (in :seon.eval/response, seon.repl/record-tx), a model call's :ms, tokens, cache hit and miss tokens and its split (:seon.model-call/*), an agent's turn (:seon.agent/turn), a task's outcome and waste (:seon.task/outcome, :seon.task/waste, waste only at the end), a process's forked and ended ms and mem-mb (:seon.process/*). Two measures already become a recorded problem at their write: :seon.turn/cache-miss (seon.turn) and :seon.process/stall (seon.problems/stalled-agents). The rest have no bound, so nothing judges them: on the standing root (2026-11-03) one eval took 2.7 GB of bytes and nothing was open. Health (seon.problems/health) lists problems only, no measures; an agent's context (seon.ctx's work view) shows its proofs, the done rule's reasons and the problems its branch caused (seon.problems/caused-split), not its measures against any bound; the model-call facts stay on the agent's branch, so main holds none (0 on the standing root after a green 3-turn run). The change, one mechanism: (1) each bound is a config key in resources/seon/config/default.edn under seon.config.*, set from measured costs (memory: limits fail loudly; no made-up limits): an eval's ticks and bytes, a run's turns and tokens, a model call's cache hit share, a process's mem-mb, an agent's wait; wall time is measured and shown, never judged (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured). (2) The writer of each measure compares it with its bound at the write and records a problem fact holding the measure and the bound, as cache-miss does: one comparison a write, nothing on an idle system, no poll and no proc. Generalize cache-miss and stall into it rather than beside them. (3) Health reads them as a recorded class; an agent's context shows the open ones on its own branch on its next turn; root's render shows all. (4) Aggregates (turns to green, agents started, tokens a task, the distribution of eval cost) are a render over the facts, drawn on read at the cost of what changed since its t (derived data cached by t, never relied on), for the dashboard and a root agent's context. (5) The run's measures reach main: the model-call facts (or their per-run sums) merge with the run, so main's aggregates see every run. Measure first: the bounds come from the distributions on a run of real tasks. Docs: docs/health.md (a measure past its bound), docs/cost.md, docs/dashboard.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/an-eval-past-its-bytes-bound-opens-a-problem seon.problems-test/a-runs-measures-reach-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-names-change"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: renames (seon.print/plain to markdown, render-ui ns, many keys) plus a chore sweep of tasks and worktrees in one task; it needs seon.ctx/teaching-is-data first and says it lands in one merge after the code settles; Context and Web agree the merge point, the owner erases the store for the stored keywords; split rename from sweep. The owner, 2026-11-05: \"Name changes too. I don't want these old ideas to come back and haunt us again\"; keep the docs and tasks updated rather than a list of what not to say. And (owner, 2026-11-06): \"yeah I always hated the text name. we need better names in general. Let's include that with the context changes so we are all on the same page and don't resurrect unclear naming\". In one merge after the code they name is settled, now that the components answer hiccup and diffs are in; it renames src/seon/render/ui.clj, so whoever is editing that file hears first. \"Text\" means three things and each gets its own name: an agent's words are ``intent`` per eval and the reply's text, its message, per turn, already so on the wire and in the db (``:seon.eval/intent``, ``:seon.model-call/reply``), so its uses in the chat and the wiki go; the printer's mark for text shown as itself against code, seon.print/plain and :seon.print/plain, becomes ``markdown``; CommonMark's sense in seon.ref's reader becomes ``text``, CommonMark's own word; the CSS class seon-text becomes ``seon-md``, beside seon-md-inline. \"Look\" is a schema's ``:seon.render/hiccup`` function. Component for part, unit and tile, :seon.render/parts included (a stored keyword: the store is erased on the owner's go); ``agent-view`` for render-ctx; ``agent-messages`` for the agent's model render; render tree for walk and walker; ``conversation``, ``turn``, ``eval-record``, ``message``, ``task``, ``cite``, ``turn-line``, ``problems``, ``proofs`` for conversation-list, chat-turn, eval-item and evaluation, chat-message, task-data, cited, run-head, own-problems and status; ``start-work`` for begin-run!; element ids by what they hold (c-rows, p-task, p-tiles, p-head, c-side); test names and :as :row, :rows, fill-rows, form-row and the like with them. Sweeps the 160 of 402 open tasks written on what has gone: rewritten as things now are, or removed when what they plan is gone. Removes jolt/render-core, jolt/insert-and-run, jolt/one-render-6-the-page-is-the-last-message and the worktrees of finished experiments once their good work has merged (a store is the owner's to reclaim). Proofs: bin/prove green; every doc and open task reads in today's names."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ctx/teaching-is-data"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "schema-entries-and-tests-cite-their-section"
  :seon.task/plan "AGENTS.md section 7 says every function, schema entry and deftest cites its wiki section, and only a function's docstring is checked (the :uncited-var class, docs/health.md). An October sweep (40 commits, 1885 behind main, dropped 2026-11-06) filled them by hand and went stale, so check first: extend the class to a schema entry's :description or leading comment and a deftest's leading comment, count what it finds on today's code, then fill those. Done means the check is red on a new uncited entry, so it stays true."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/schema-entry-cited-wiki-section-description-deftest-none}}

 {:seon.task/name "a-set-holding-nil-called-as-a-function-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the Tests session on jolt/tests-items (2026-11-11). ``(#{nil :deferred} status)`` answers the element it finds, so for a nil status it answers nil, which reads as false: ```seon.agent/judge!``` and ```seon.agent/with-gate-verdicts``` took a test with no :seon.test/status for a judged one, so a process proof the gate never ran read as reused and green (seon.agent-test/a-process-test-gets-its-verdict-while-the-agent-can-act, red once its run stopped writing :deferred first). The two sites now say ``(contains? #{nil :deferred} status)``. What allowed it: nothing refuses a set literal holding nil or false in a call's head, though it can never answer true for that element. Correct: a derived class of ```seon.problems/health```, read from the compiler's facts as :unnamed-site is (docs/health.md#checks-are-callers-of-seon): each form calling a set literal (or a set def'd as a value) whose elements include nil or false is a problem naming its var, so the class cannot land again. The sweep on 2026-11-11 found these two sites only (src and test)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-set-holding-nil-called-as-a-function-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wiki-line-naming-what-no-longer-exists-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-11 (by Point): \"I want to move away from just putting all the information in one place and into distributing the updates to bring the system up to date before each session is concluded. Over time I want to stop drift.\" A session's sweep before it concludes is the rule; this is its script. What exists: a task's plan cites in triple backticks, and a dead cite is refused at its write (seon.task); a wiki section cites vars the same way (:seon.doc/names), and ```seon.problems/stale-sections``` makes a :stale-section row when a cited var's contract or docstring changed since the section was read. Nothing finds a wiki line naming a var that is gone, a config key no schema declares, a bin/check step ```seon.proofs/check-steps``` does not list, or a bin/ script or file that is not there: the sweep of 2026-11-11 found seon.prove's ns doc citing docs/proofs.md#the-test-service, a page that does not exist. Correct: a derived class of ```seon.problems/health``` beside :stale-section, read from the facts it already holds (the sections' names, the program's vars, the config schema's keys, check-steps, the tree's files at the checkout): each name in a section, a docstring or a schema :description that resolves to nothing is a problem row naming the line's address and the name, so its fix is the page edit. A docs/<page>.md#<anchor> address cited anywhere (src, test, the wiki, a task's plan) resolves to a heading of that page (seon.doc's anchors), not only to the page: Optimization's helper cited docs/merging.md#the-merge-gate, which laws and health passed and only a review caught (2026-11-11). A fenced form in a section whose head names a var is that var at an arity it has; evaluating a whole example form needs a world, so it is a test over the sections (seon.doc-test's), not health: the owner's round ruled every wiki example form evaluates (Point, 2026-11-11, Tests with Data model). Names in single backticks that are not a ns/var, :ns/key, bin/ path or docs/ address are words, never read. Cost: one pass over the sections' text and the descriptions at health (docs/health.md#cost). Proof: a section naming a removed var, an undeclared :seon.config key, an unlisted step and a missing docs/ page is four rows; the wiki at main is none, or each row is a fix landed with the task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-wiki-line-naming-what-no-longer-exists-is-a-problem}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-entry-that-says-nothing-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: a public function whose docstring cites no section is a problem of the branch that changed it (```seon.problems/uncited-vars```, docs/health.md#a-function-that-cites-no-section); a schema entry with no `:description` is nothing, and about 130 attributes of the entity schemas said what they are only in comments until branch jolt/descriptions. A count pinned in a test was tried and dropped: a second mechanism beside this one, with a number kept by hand. Plan: the same class for a schema entry, judged on what a branch changed: an entry of a schema file the branch added or changed that has no `:description` is a row, closed by writing one; added to the classes a branch is judged on. Cost: one seek a changed entry. Proof, red first: a branch that adds an attribute with no description has one row naming it; with a description, none; an entry the branch did not touch is not read."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-changed-schema-entry-with-no-description-is-a-row}}
 {:seon.task/name "health-costs-what-the-merge-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: tests. Found 2026-11-12 (tests, a whole prove after a merge of main): bin/check health ran STEP-OVERRAN at its 86.3 s bound (the step-end factor times its median, seon.proofs/step-bounds) at load 14; its sample shows the main thread working, not waiting. On jolt/tests its wall the same day ranged 21 s to 218 s: a run on a tree whose derived problems were made before is fast, the first run after a merge does the whole program's work again. So the bound is right and the cost is wrong (AGENTS.md section 3a.2, proportional to what changed): a merge of d changed forms must cost health O(d). Plan: measure first where the cold run's ticks go (the program db is already brought current by seon.source/refresh-tx from the nearest kept tree, test-runner/program-db, so look at what health derives over it: seon.problems' derived rows, the reach graph, the wiki's citations), estimate each part's lower bound from the forms the merge changed, then derive each from the previous tree's result plus the changed forms' facts, keyed by digest. Do not raise the bound. Proof, red first: health on a tree one changed form past a tree it already judged spends at most 10 times that form's own share of ticks, not the whole program's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/health-after-a-one-form-change-costs-that-form}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hand-written-list-of-parts-is-a-problem"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner (2026-11-10 \"NO HAND WRITTEN LISTS\"; 2026-11-14: \"If we can make a law or a check that fires or via the problems I want this enforced\"); root approved this design 2026-11-14. A page's parts come from what the entity's pull holds, each joined entity drawn by its schema's render (seon.render.ui/tiles names no part); a render that is a page list (:seon.render/list metadata) and names other components in its code is a hand-written list. Measured 2026-11-14 in src/seon/render/ui.clj: seven lists name parts: agent-parts (status, own-problems, ctx-keys, turn, agent-state, eval-tiles), conversation-list (chat-ask, chat-end, conversation-turn, examples, proof-story, system-message), rules-list, unresolved-list, stage-list, pull-list, agents-list; root's page names five. The change: a derived problem class in seon.problems (the program index holds each var's :seon.render/list metadata and each form's :form/refs), one row per list naming a component render; a branch that adds one causes it, so the done rule's :open-problem refuses it (no waiver); each existing row is a task (web's for the page lists, context's for agent-parts: each named part's render declared on the data it draws, so the walk matches it). Calls of helpers that are no component (markdown) are not parts. Proof: a branch defining a :seon.render/list render that names a component is caused the problem; one drawing its parts from the pull is not. Estimate: one read of the lists' forms' refs, O(lists)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.problems-test/a-list-naming-its-parts-is-a-problem}}
]
